<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Something went wrong</title>
  <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.8/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/fse.css">
</head>
<body class="fse-body">

  <main class="container fse-form-wrap">
    <div class="card shadow-sm p-4 p-md-5 mx-auto fse-card fse-card-wide">

      <div class="text-center mb-4">
        <div class="display-1 fw-bold text-danger mb-2"><%= status %></div>
        <h1 class="h3 fw-bold mb-2">There is an error</h1>
        <p class="text-secondary mb-0"><%= message %></p>
      </div>

      <div class="alert alert-light border mb-4">
        <div class="small text-secondary mb-1">The request that failed</div>
        <code class="fs-6"><%= method %> <%= path %></code>
      </div>

      <p class="text-secondary">
        A <strong>404</strong> from an API route does not mean the server is down &mdash;
        the server answered. It means no handler matched
        <em>that method on that path</em>. The browser sent one verb; the
        server is listening for a different one.
      </p>

      <p class="text-secondary mb-4">
        Open DevTools &rarr; Network, click the failed request, and compare
        the <strong>Request Method</strong> against what
        <code>server/routers/attendance.router.js</code> declares.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-0 text-center">
        <a href="/attendance" class="link-secondary small">Back to the form</a>
        <span class="text-secondary small mx-2">&middot;</span>
        <a href="/" class="link-secondary small">Home</a>
      </p>

    </div>
  </main>

</body>
</html>
